 ## Consequences
@@ -27,4 +28,5 @@ The library holds material governed by IRB, GDPR, HIPAA and funder rules. Every
 - The project name in the move reason is the request's `projectId` until the request carries a name.
 - The repository (`packages/library/src/repo/mutate.ts`) audits every allow and deny decision for an existing project. The one exception is `createProject`: its decision is made before the project, and so its audit log, exists. A deny there throws before anything is written and is not audited; an allow is recorded as the first event of the new log.
 - Repository writes use a write-ahead marker (`<Library>/.tmp/txn-<id>.json`, see `repo/txn.ts`). Every allow event of a write carries `details.txn`. If a crash interrupts a write before all its allow events are appended, the next writable open rolls the write back and reports `rolled-back-incomplete-write`. Data never stays live without its audit record.
+- Backups are governed like every other action. Manual and scheduled backups alike are evaluated before anything is written, and a deny is audited (`backup`, with the reason) and creates nothing. Backups are not an exception to the write-ahead rule, but they cannot use the txn marker: the marker may only touch the project's own folder, and a backup is written to `<Library>/Backups/`. They follow the same rule with a record of their own instead, kept in `Backups/backups-state.json` (`pending`). The allow decision is audited first (`backup`, with its conditions and a `backupId`). The zip is then written in full under a temp name and fsynced. **The commit point is the `backup.create` audit event** carrying the same `backupId`. Only after that event is the file renamed to its backup name and listed in the manifest. On the next writable open, and before every backup operation, an interrupted backup is settled by what the audit log says. If the log holds `backup.create`, the backup is renamed and listed. If it does not, the temp file is overwritten and removed and the project shows `backup-failed`. A backup is never named or listed as one without its event. If the event cannot be written, the backup is not kept and `backup-failed` gives the reason (R28, R82). Removing old backups is a plain delete (R91). It is recorded in `pending` before the first file goes and audited afterwards, as one `backup.prune` event naming the files. If a crash or a failed append loses that event, it is written on the next settle (`recovered: true`), and the project shows `backup-audit-pending` until then.
 - An audit event or change exists only once its whole line and newline are durably written (R28). A crash during an append outside a transaction (a deny event, an unlock, an integrity event written at open) can leave an incomplete last line and no marker. The next writable open removes such a line from a project's last audit segment and its change logs, reports `trimmed-incomplete-audit-line`, and records `audit.trimmed-incomplete-line` with the file, byte count and SHA-256 of the removed bytes. A read-only open, or a project with an unresolved marker, only reports it (`incomplete-audit-line-pending`). An audit append that fails while opening blocks that project only, never the whole library.
diff --git a/docs/governance/README.md b/docs/governance/README.md
index 6b6be60..e10c02a 100644
--- a/docs/governance/README.md
+++ b/docs/governance/README.md
@@ -77,6 +77,18 @@ Phones and many cameras record where a photo, video or voice memo was made (GPS)
 - If the media helper is not running, the original is still stored as above, and the source shows that its viewing copy is pending ("proxy pending"). The copy is made, with the rule applied, as soon as the media helper is available.
 - Voice memos and other recordings have no viewing copy; their location stays only in the original, stored under the same rule.
 
+### Backups
+
+A backup is a full copy of one project: every file, the audit log included. The app writes it to the `Backups` folder inside the library, so it is stored wherever the library is stored. Backups are made by hand or once a day automatically. A project's rules decide each backup in the same way as they decide storing the project's data in that place. Backups have no setting of their own.
+
+- **Where the library is.** The `Backups` folder must be on a kind of storage the project allows under "where data may live" (`residency.allowed`). The app checks this for the folder's path and also for the place it really points to. A cloud-synced folder must also be on the project's approved list when HIPAA applies, or whenever that list is not empty. If the app cannot tell what kind of storage the folder is on, it checks every kind it might be, and refuses the backup if any of them is not allowed.
+- **Encryption.** In an encrypted project the whole backup is sealed with the project's key, and no unencrypted copy reaches the disk. When a project's rules require encryption at rest (`encryption.atRest: required`, which HIPAA always sets), the project never gets an unencrypted backup. If such a project is not encrypted, its backups are refused.
+- **When it is refused.** Nothing is created, and the researcher sees the reason in plain words, for example: "Backups are kept in the library's folder, which is on a network drive; this project's rules do not allow its data there." When the rules cannot be read, every backup is refused. If an automatic backup is refused or fails, the project shows a warning that the last backup failed, with the reason. After a refusal the app asks again a day later; after a failure, at its next hourly check. The warning stays until a backup succeeds.
+- **What is recorded.** Every backup decision goes into the project's audit log, refusals included, with the reason. When a backup is allowed, the log first records the decision and any condition on it (encryption). It then records the finished backup: its file name, size, SHA-256 fingerprint and number of files. A backup counts as made only once that second entry is written. If the app is interrupted before then, the unfinished file is overwritten and removed the next time the library opens, and the project shows that the backup failed. If the entry cannot be written, the backup is not kept. No path is recorded, only the file name.
+- **Removing old backups.** The app keeps the newest backups of each project (10 unless the project says otherwise) and deletes older ones with an ordinary delete. It deletes only files listed as this project's backups. Each removal goes into the audit log with the names of the deleted files. If that entry cannot be written straight away, the project shows a warning until it is.
+- **Restoring** a backup makes a new project and records the restore, with the backup's fingerprint, in the new project's audit log.
+- **Copies elsewhere.** Every backup the app makes stays in the library's `Backups` folder. This lets destroying raw data under the retention rule find every copy and list it on the destruction certificate. The app does not make backups in any other place. To keep a copy safe from the loss of the drive, copy the whole library folder yourself. That copy is outside the app's records: retention cannot destroy it, and you are responsible for it.
+
 ## Enforcement status
 
 At present the workbench computes the effective policy and the list of violations. Actually blocking actions (for example refusing a capture until a protocol is recorded) lands with the policy engine, the next task. Where the profile pages say the app "blocks" something, that describes the behavior the policy engine will enforce.
